swer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388503 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388504 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388506 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388507 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388508 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388510 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388511 :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186388511 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 8-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{68}\\begin{bmatrix}68 \\\\ -68\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388513 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388514 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388515 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388516 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388517 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388519 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388520 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388521 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388522 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388523 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186388524 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\