value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390756 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390757 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$"] [17592186390757 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{9}{2}.$"] [17592186390777 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186390777 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390779 :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\frac{9}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [17592186390780 :answer/value "x(t)=-\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [17592186390781 :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{9}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}"] [17592186390782 :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [17592186390783 :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}"] [17592186390784 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$*"] [17592186390784 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=-2{e}^{4} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3}{c}_{1}=3{e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3}(e)+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390786 :answer/value "x(t)=-2{e}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-3e\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390787 :answer/value "x(t)=2e\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}-3{e}^{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390788 :answer/value "x(t)=e\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390789 :answer/value "x(t)=2e\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390790 :answer/value "x(t)=2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390791 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${f}_{13}$ in  $F=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186390791 :question/worked-solution "To get the minor of ${f}_{13}$, we remove the $1$st row and $3$rd column:\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 5 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 7 & ∗ & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & ∗ & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & ∗ & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the minor is\n\n$$\n{M}_{13}=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186390806 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390807 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390808 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390809 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390810 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390939 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=5{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186390955 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=2{X}_{1}(s)+6{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."] [17592186390971 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =5x-y \\\\  & =5(1)-(-1) \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x-2y \\\\  & =-1-2(-1) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186390973 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390974 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390975 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390976 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390977 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390978 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186390978 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 192\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390980 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390981 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390982 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390983 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 192\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186390984 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}192 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"]}
